WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.800
<v Stephane>So now let's talk about STS,</v>

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.220
which is a service that is so important in AWS,

3
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.340
which isn't going to allow us to assume roles

4
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.520
across different accounts within the same accounts,

5
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.670
and also enable us to do identity federation,

6
00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.170
so let's get started.

7
00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.080
First, we define IAM role within your accounts

8
00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.600
or across accounts that we want to assume.

9
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.140
Then we define which principals can access this IAM role,

10
00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.760
and we'll use the STS service,

11
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.640
which stands for Security Token Service,

12
00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.720
to retrieve credentials,

13
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.250
and they're gonna be temporary,

14
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.240
and this will allow us

15
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.940
to impersonate the IAM role we have access to

16
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.220
using the AssumeRole API,

17
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.440
and the credentials are going to be temporary, as I said,

18
00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.020
between 15 minutes to 12 hours,

19
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.490
so the diagram, what does it look like?

20
00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:46.410
Our users wants to access a role,

21
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.320
for example, it's within the same or another accounts,

22
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:50.370
and so, to do so,

23
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.730
it's going to use the AssumeRole API on STS.

24
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.770
STS will check the permissions

25
00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.030
and make sure the setup is correct,

26
00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.770
and we'll go into details on how that look likes,

27
00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.260
and then, we'll get security credentials back.

28
00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.610
They're gonna be temporary,

29
00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.350
and these security credentials

30
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.780
will allow us to access the target role,

31
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.070
so in which situations

32
00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.680
would we want to assume a role using STS?

33
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.513
Well, first of all,

34
00:01:11.513 --> 00:01:13.890
if you want to provide access to an IAM user

35
00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.920
in an AWS account that you own or your own accounts

36
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:18.360
to another resource,

37
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.880
or maybe you want to provide IAM access users

38
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:21.870
in other accounts,

39
00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.060
third parties access to your account as well.

40
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.620
Maybe you want to also

41
00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.780
help your AWS services do what they need,

42
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.550
so using service roles,

43
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.700
and then, maybe we also want to perform identity federation,

44
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.550
and we'll have a deep dive onto identity federation

45
00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:36.383
in this course

46
00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:37.980
because you need to know them all.

47
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.160
Now, for STS, there's this feature

48
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.590
which allows you to revoke active sessions

49
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.210
and credentials for a role,

50
00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.710
and so this is very simple.

51
00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:49.590
You just need to change the policy and add a time statement

52
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:53.340
or use the AWSRevokeOlderSessions manage policy,

53
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.600
and that's perfect,

54
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.400
and so using this time condition,

55
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.233
you're able to say,

56
00:01:57.233 --> 00:01:59.280
"Okay, this role cannot be used anymore,"

57
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.220
and so that gives you an extra level of security.

58
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.130
Remember, when we use STS, when we assume a role

59
00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.350
for it be a user, an application, or a service,

60
00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.140
we give up our original permissions,

61
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.210
and we take the permissions assigned to the role,

62
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.680
and so we already had a discussion

63
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.870
as to whether we should use a role

64
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.610
or a resource-based policy,

65
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.650
but just a slight reminder here.

66
00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.140
Okay, so here is the deep dive a little bit

67
00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.150
on providing access to an IAM user

68
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.580
in your or another AWS accounts that you own,

69
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.520
so this is within your circle of trust,

70
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.680
in which case, we can, for example,

71
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:33.900
create a role in Account A or A*.

72
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.000
It could be another account that you own, okay?

73
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.580
To terminate EC2 instances,

74
00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.020
and so this is very important

75
00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.520
that this is all the accounts that you own

76
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.660
because in the next slide,

77
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:45.420
we'll see about the accounts we don't own, third parties,

78
00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.120
so in this case, the user will assume a role,

79
00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.860
and that role will be allowing us

80
00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.350
to terminate EC2 instances,

81
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:53.280
and the benefits is that,

82
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.620
is that we need to explicitly grant our users

83
00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.480
permission to assume the role

84
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.170
to perform the Terminate EC2 Instance action.

85
00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.980
We can also force the users to switch to that role

86
00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.300
using the Management Console

87
00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.940
or the CLI and the AssumeRole API,

88
00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:09.773
and so the idea

89
00:03:09.773 --> 00:03:12.480
is that we have an extra level of security here.

90
00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.420
We can also add multifactor authentication protection

91
00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.253
to the role

92
00:03:16.253 --> 00:03:19.050
so that only users are assigned using MFA

93
00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:20.820
can assume the role,

94
00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.680
and so what this gives us, as well, is

95
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.130
least privilege and auditing using CloudTrail,

96
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.310
so by using this intermediary step

97
00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.620
to do terminate EC2 instances,

98
00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.500
we have a lot of extra security added to it,

99
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.960
and that is a very common pattern in AWS,

100
00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.640
something that a solutions architect

101
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.980
should be thinking about a lot.

102
00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:43.680
Okay, but what does it look like in practice

103
00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.020
when we have STS cross-account access?

104
00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:46.920
Again, just a reminder,

105
00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.990
but something you've already seen before, probably,

106
00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.360
so we have a production accounts with an S3 buckets

107
00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.000
and a development accounts with two groups of users,

108
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.160
for example, testers and developers,

109
00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.110
and we want to provide developers

110
00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.080
access to the S3 bucket production app.

111
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.120
Okay, so the first thing we do is that

112
00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.440
in the production accounts,

113
00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.180
the admin will create a role

114
00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.740
that grants the development account

115
00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.470
read/write access to the productionapp bucket,

116
00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.380
so this role will allow us to access the S3 buckets.

117
00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:14.910
Then, in the development accounts,

118
00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:19.050
we're granting the members of the developer group access

119
00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.650
to assume this UpdateApp role,

120
00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.090
so what happens then?

121
00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.590
The users of that group,

122
00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.120
the developers group only,

123
00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.897
can access or request access to the role using the STS API,

124
00:04:30.897 --> 00:04:34.590
and STS will return the role temporary credentials,

125
00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:35.970
and using these credentials,

126
00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.500
the users can access the S3 bucket

127
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:38.790
using the role credentials,

128
00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.380
so very, very, very simple.

129
00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.250
Again, just revision,

130
00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.880
but I want to make dead clear how STS works

131
00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.370
because it gets a little bit more complicated right now,

132
00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.260
and this is probably something new for you,

133
00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:51.990
but very important to note going into the exam,

134
00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.720
so in case you want to provide access

135
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.390
to AWS accounts owned by third parties,

136
00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:00.240
there's going to be an extra thing called external ID,

137
00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.280
so this is text, and then I have a diagram,

138
00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.690
so there's a zone of trust,

139
00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:04.590
and the zone of trust

140
00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.560
is all the accounts and organizations that you own,

141
00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.450
and outside of that zone of trust

142
00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:10.890
is going to be third parties.

143
00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.660
For example, you work with a partner,

144
00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.980
and they provide you a service,

145
00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:16.650
and you want to give, like I say, consulting company,

146
00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.450
and you want to give that consulting company

147
00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:19.920
access to your accounts.

148
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.630
They're outside of your zone of trust.

149
00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:25.230
They're not directly in your organization,

150
00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.890
and so you can use IAM Access Analyzer

151
00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.500
to find out which resources in your accounts

152
00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.540
are outside of your zone of trust, okay?

153
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:36.030
Which account, which resources, for example, your S3 buckets

154
00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:38.580
are going to be granted to third parties,

155
00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:40.620
and so if you wanna grant access to your third parties

156
00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.210
to some of your resources,

157
00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.360
you can get something called the third-party AWS account ID,

158
00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:47.970
which is a very obvious,

159
00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.700
and the most important thing here is

160
00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.040
you define an external ID,

161
00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.080
and this external ID is a secret

162
00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.970
between you and the third party,

163
00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.980
and you have to define it,

164
00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.250
and this is because if you give access to the third party

165
00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.780
to a role in your accounts,

166
00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:05.670
you want to make sure that only that third party

167
00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.290
has access to this role,

168
00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.030
so this is to uniquely associate the role

169
00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:10.590
between you and a third party,

170
00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:12.720
and you must be provided when defining the trust

171
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:14.070
and when assuming the role,

172
00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:15.240
and we'll see why,

173
00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.310
and it must be chosen by the third party.

174
00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:20.490
You define the permissions in the IAM policy,

175
00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:21.720
and you're good to go,

176
00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:23.910
so let's have a look at a bit more details

177
00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:25.470
to make this a bit more clear

178
00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:28.890
about this, why this external ID is so important,

179
00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.050
and this is called the confused deputy,

180
00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:34.620
so this is a diagram directly coming from the documentation,

181
00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.040
and so we see here is that we have your AWS accounts

182
00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:39.450
on the left-hand side,

183
00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.880
and we have the third party in the middle,

184
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.710
and we have another AWS accounts

185
00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.510
on the right-hand side, okay?

186
00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.850
And what the account on the right-hand side

187
00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:48.810
that we don't own

188
00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.570
is going to try to attack us on the left-hand side,

189
00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:52.410
and to attack us,

190
00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.990
it's going to be using the account in the middle,

191
00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.990
so let's see what happens when you don't use the external ID

192
00:06:57.990 --> 00:06:59.820
and how this attack is possible

193
00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:02.430
and what happens when the, once we have the external ID,

194
00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:04.410
and how this prevents this attack,

195
00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:07.440
so in the first case, our AWS accounts right here

196
00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.110
has created an ExampleRole right here,

197
00:07:10.110 --> 00:07:13.170
and this ExampleRole is going to be able to be assumed

198
00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:14.940
by the middle accounts,

199
00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.170
so we're saying, "Okay, here is the ARN of this ExampleRole,

200
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:20.820
and you are able to assume this role

201
00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.520
and access some of our resources within our accounts

202
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.353
using this role,

203
00:07:24.353 --> 00:07:26.640
so this is everything we've seen so far,

204
00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.920
and then, the other AWS accounts

205
00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.810
is going to try to attack us,

206
00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:32.977
so it's going to say, "Hey," to the middle accounts,

207
00:07:32.977 --> 00:07:34.687
"Hey, I have a role for you,

208
00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.097
"and this role is going to allow you

209
00:07:36.097 --> 00:07:38.340
"to access stuff in my accounts,"

210
00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.860
but instead of providing a role in their accounts,

211
00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:45.000
they provide the exact same AWS1 ExampleRole ARN

212
00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.410
to the middle accounts,

213
00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:47.243
so this one doesn't know.

214
00:07:47.243 --> 00:07:48.750
It can't verify whether this role

215
00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.330
belongs to the accounts on the left or on the right,

216
00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.610
and so it's going to assume that role,

217
00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:54.900
and by assuming that role,

218
00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:55.910
instead of assuming the role

219
00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:56.823
on the right-hand side accounts,

220
00:07:56.823 --> 00:07:58.100
it's going to assume roles

221
00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.540
in the left-hand side accounts without knowing it,

222
00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:01.680
and so, therefore,

223
00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.140
it's going to think that it's going to be performing actions

224
00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:05.400
on the right-hand side accounts,

225
00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.870
but actually, it performs action

226
00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:08.070
on the left-hand side accounts,

227
00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.810
and this is called the confused deputy

228
00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.300
because the account in the middle is confused.

229
00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.770
It thinks it's going to operate

230
00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:14.910
on the right-hand side accounts,

231
00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:17.097
but it actually operates on the left-hand side accounts,

232
00:08:17.097 --> 00:08:21.450
and so this is why AWS defined this external ID thing,

233
00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.580
and let's see how this protects against this attack,

234
00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:24.660
and this is an exam question,

235
00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:27.450
so this is why I'm going go deep into this,

236
00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.120
so we have now the same setup,

237
00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.150
but this time, we also define an external ID

238
00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:35.280
to assume this role,

239
00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.920
and this external ID is something secret

240
00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.810
that is shared between your accounts

241
00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.520
and the account in the middle,

242
00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.353
so we're saying,

243
00:08:42.353 --> 00:08:45.270
"Okay, here is the ARN of the account you should assume,

244
00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:47.730
but when you do assume this ARN,

245
00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:50.130
you need to pass in the external ID

246
00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.080
that we have defined together,

247
00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.360
and so, as such, okay, it's able to access this role

248
00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.660
because we know we both know the secrets, okay?

249
00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.180
And when we look at the other AWS accounts

250
00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.220
on the right-hand side, which is trying to attack us,

251
00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.690
it's going to try to use, again,

252
00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:05.280
this role on the left-hand side,

253
00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.040
but it doesn't know the external ID,

254
00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.900
and so it's going to give another external ID

255
00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.670
to the account in the middle,

256
00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:14.550
and this time, by providing the wrong external ID,

257
00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.950
this is going to be preventing the account in the middle

258
00:09:16.950 --> 00:09:18.450
to access our AWS accounts

259
00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.610
because the external ID is not matching,

260
00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:24.060
and therefore, we have added an extra level of security,

261
00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:25.110
so bottom line is

262
00:09:25.110 --> 00:09:26.670
you don't need to know exactly all the steps,

263
00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.170
but I want to show you exactly how that works

264
00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.150
and not just tell you using external ID

265
00:09:30.150 --> 00:09:31.590
prevents this kind of attacks,

266
00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.240
so bottom line is still

267
00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.910
if you are using third-party accounts,

268
00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.520
and they need to get access to your AWS accounts,

269
00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.140
and you need to secure that,

270
00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.290
the answer is to use the STS AssumeRole API,

271
00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:45.600
but also to define an external ID,

272
00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:46.560
and this, hopefully,

273
00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.230
description of how this works helps you.

274
00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:52.230
You can also use session tags in STS.

275
00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:55.200
The idea is that when you assume an IAM role,

276
00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:56.033
or, for example,

277
00:09:56.033 --> 00:09:58.500
you have user federation that leverages STS,

278
00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:01.770
it is possible for you to pass a session tag,

279
00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:02.910
so how does that work?

280
00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:04.710
Well, your user is going to, for example,

281
00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.620
do the STS AssumeRole API call

282
00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:10.260
and pass a session tag as part of the API call,

283
00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:13.470
for example, for Department=HR,

284
00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:14.970
and STS will then return

285
00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.550
temporary security credentials for that user

286
00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:19.140
with the session tag,

287
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:20.490
so why do we do this?

288
00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:23.400
Well, then, in our IAM policies,

289
00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.286
we can use a condition called the aws:PrincipalTag,

290
00:10:27.286 --> 00:10:29.100
and the idea is that using this,

291
00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:31.980
the tag of the condition is going to be compared

292
00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:35.550
to the tag passed to the STS AssumeRole,

293
00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:38.490
and so this allows us to have conditions

294
00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:40.860
to make sure that the principal making the request

295
00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:42.660
has the specified tags.

296
00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.960
For example, we have an S3 bucket named hr-docs,

297
00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:49.170
and it contains this S3 bucket policy.

298
00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:50.970
As you can see in the condition,

299
00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:55.470
there is a StringEquals PrincipalTag/Department equals HR,

300
00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:57.060
and therefore, only a user

301
00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:00.840
that has done an AssumeRole with that specific session tag

302
00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:04.350
will be able to make an API call into the S3 buckets,

303
00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:06.180
so this is particularly helpful

304
00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.220
when you federate users in STS

305
00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:12.240
and you want to have some conditions in your IAM policies

306
00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.093
based on some tags passed through the federation,

307
00:11:15.960 --> 00:11:18.000
so finally, to conclude with STS,

308
00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.040
there's a few important APIs you need to know.

309
00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.050
The AssumRole is the most basic one,

310
00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:23.190
which is to access a role

311
00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:25.464
within your account or across accounts.

312
00:11:25.464 --> 00:11:27.000
AssumeRoleWithSAML,

313
00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.020
which is to get credentials when we are federated with SAML.

314
00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.610
AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity,

315
00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:34.800
which we are using a IdP,

316
00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:37.740
and so that includes Amazon Cognito, Login with Amazon,

317
00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:41.640
Facebook, Google, or any OpenID Connect-compatible IdP,

318
00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.710
and so we'll be seeing this in detail

319
00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:45.570
in the federation section,

320
00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:47.640
and overall, just so you know right now,

321
00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:48.690
AWS does not recommend

322
00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:51.840
to use the AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity API anymore.

323
00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:54.270
It recommends to use Cognito instead.

324
00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:57.120
Then we have a GetSessionToken, which is used for MFA,

325
00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:00.090
so when we log using MFA to get credentials back,

326
00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:01.710
or GetFederationToken,

327
00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:05.520
which is usually to get a credentials,

328
00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:06.353
temporary credentials,

329
00:12:06.353 --> 00:12:08.760
when we have federation using a proxy app,

330
00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:11.100
for example, when we have something that distributes tokens

331
00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:13.470
within a corporate network, okay?

332
00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:14.520
So at a high level,

333
00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:16.560
these are the important APIs you need to know.

334
00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.720
The most important ones are going to be the first four.

335
00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.940
I don't think the fifth one is going to be in the exam,

336
00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.850
but still, for me, good to tell you what it is, okay?

337
00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:24.683
So that's it.

338
00:12:24.683 --> 00:12:25.620
I will see you in the next lecture

339
00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.183
for some identity federation.

